TASK 12 – Re-evaluate and Adapt the Design (8 Marks)
A. Impact analysis (2 marks)
The original design calculates a monthly bill using previous and current meter readings. The new smart-meter system sends readings in watt-hours every 15 minutes, applies a peak-hour tariff from 6 PM to 10 PM, and must allow tariff changes without recompiling.

The design should be modified into an interval-based billing system.
Original design	New requirement	Impact
Two monthly readings	35,040 readings per house per year	Store timestamped interval readings
uint16_t meter readings	Watt-hour values and cumulative yearly energy	Use wider integer types
Fixed #define tariff rates	Tariffs change at runtime	Read and validate a configuration file
One energy rate	Peak-hour rate is 20% higher	Classify each interval by time
Integer paise totals	Many interval charges accumulated	Use wide integer arithmetic and explicit rounding

Largest values
Assuming 35,040 intervals per year and a maximum 16-bit watt-hour value per interval:
\[
35,040 \times 65,535
= 2,295,986,400 \text{ Wh}
\]

This is approximately 2.296 billion Wh, or 2,295,986.4 kWh, per house per year under that theoretical maximum.

A 32-bit signed int cannot safely store this Wh total because its maximum is 2,147,483,647. A 64-bit integer can store it safely.

The actual meter specification must determine the maximum Wh value per interval; the calculation above assumes each interval reading itself is limited to 65,535 Wh.
B. Evaluation of the original design (2 marks)
The original design is partly suitable, but it cannot be retained unchanged.
Original feature	Evaluation	Revised choice
uint16_t	Suitable only if an individual interval's Wh value fits within 65,535	Keep only if confirmed by the meter specification
uint32_t yearly Wh total	Insufficient for the theoretical maximum of 2,295,986,400 Wh per year	Use uint64_t
uint64_t for money	Suitable for annual and district totals, subject to overflow checks	Retain
#define RATE2_PAISE 225	Requires recompilation to change	Load rates from a configuration file
Fixed slab if statements	Do not support peak and off-peak periods by themselves	Add interval classification
Integer paise	Prevents floating-point addition errors	Retain, but accumulate fractional-paise calculations before rounding

The 20% peak increase can be represented as a multiplier of 120/100. Because a 15-minute interval may produce a fractional-paise charge, the revised program should accumulate charges as integer numerators and round the final energy charge to the nearest paise.

CODE:


#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <inttypes.h>

#define MAX_INTERVALS 35040U
#define MAX_INTERVAL_WH 65535U
#define PEAK_MULTIPLIER 120ULL
#define NORMAL_MULTIPLIER 100ULL
#define RATE_LIMIT 1000000ULL
#define FIXED_LIMIT 1000000000000ULL

int main(void)
{
    FILE *file = fopen("tariff.txt", "r");
    unsigned long long rate, fixed;
    uint64_t total_numerator = 0;
    uint64_t energy_paise, total;
    unsigned int n, hour, wh;

    if (file == NULL) {
        printf("Cannot open tariff.txt\n");
        return 1;
    }

    if (fscanf(file,
        "base_rate_paise_per_kwh=%llu\n"
        "fixed_charge_paise=%llu",
        &rate, &fixed) != 2 ||
        rate > RATE_LIMIT || fixed > FIXED_LIMIT) {
        printf("Invalid tariff configuration\n");
        fclose(file);
        return 1;
    }
    fclose(file);

    printf("Enter number of intervals (1-%u): ", MAX_INTERVALS);

    if (scanf("%u", &n) != 1 ||
        n == 0 || n > MAX_INTERVALS) {
        printf("Invalid interval count\n");
        return 1;
    }

    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("Enter hour (0-23) and energy (Wh): ");

        if (scanf("%u %u", &hour, &wh) != 2 ||
            hour > 23 || wh > MAX_INTERVAL_WH) {
            printf("Invalid interval data\n");
            return 1;
        }

        uint64_t multiplier =
            (hour >= 18 && hour < 22)
            ? PEAK_MULTIPLIER : NORMAL_MULTIPLIER;

        /* Numerator denominator = 100,000:
           Wh * paise/kWh * multiplier / 100,000 */
        total_numerator +=
            (uint64_t)wh * rate * multiplier;
    }

    /* Round energy charge once to nearest paise */
    energy_paise = (total_numerator + 50000ULL) / 100000ULL;
    total = energy_paise + fixed;

    printf("\n--- Smart Meter Bill ---\n");
    printf("Energy charge: Rs. %" PRIu64 ".%02" PRIu64 "\n",
           energy_paise / 100, energy_paise % 100);
    printf("Fixed charge: Rs. %" PRIu64 ".%02" PRIu64 "\n",
           fixed / 100, fixed % 100);
    printf("Total bill: Rs. %" PRIu64 ".%02" PRIu64 "\n",
           total / 100, total % 100);

    return 0;
}

SAMPLE INPUT AND OUTPUT:

Enter number of intervals (1-35040): 4
Enter hour (0-23) and energy (Wh): 17 1000
Enter hour (0-23) and energy (Wh): 18 1000
Enter hour (0-23) and energy (Wh): 21 500
Enter hour (0-23) and energy (Wh): 22 500

OUTPUT:

--- Smart Meter Bill ---
Energy charge: Rs. 7.43
Fixed charge: Rs. 50.00
Total bill: Rs. 57.43




